<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=outrigger-canoe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Outrigger canoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000 BCE</span> · Also known as Outrigger boat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An outrigger canoe is a boat steadied by one or more side floats, or outriggers, fastened to its hull; such boats were developed by the Austronesian-speaking peoples of the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and they enabled the Austronesian expansion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> from around 3000 BCE. Outrigger and double-hulled canoes carried Austronesians east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Through early Austronesian contact, estimated at around 1000–600 BCE and later, they spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and they also reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe.jpg" alt="Outrigger canoe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borobudur_-_Divyavadana_-_086_N,_The_Landing_of_Hiru_and_foundation_of_Hiruka_(detail_3)_(11706466016).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>No Lapita canoe survives, but the speed and reach of their voyages, the Proto-Oceanic words that linguists reconstruct for outriggers, sails and paddles, and the later canoes of Oceania show that they built <b>outrigger canoes</b>, and perhaps double-hulled ones, able to cross hundreds of kilometres of open sea.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Island Southeast Asia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Outriggers may first have been needed after the crab claw sail appeared, around 1500 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=outrigger-canoe">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
